), you can consider dropping them.\n\n```python\n# Dropping 'YearsEmployed', 'Employed', and 'PriorDefault' since they are captured by 'Employment_Stability_Score'\ndf.drop(columns=['YearsEmployed', 'Employed', 'PriorDefault'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping 'Age' since it is adequately represented by the binned 'Age_Group'.\ndf.drop(columns=['Age'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\nBy adding features like `Debt_to_Income_Ratio`, categorical binning for `Age_Group`, and creating a composite score like `Employment_Stability_Score`, these engineered features will likely increase the separability of data points and help the downstream model learn more nuanced patterns.", "```python\n# Feature Name: Debt_to_Income_Ratio\n# Feature Description: Create a new feature that calculates the ratio of Debt to Income for each individual. \n# Formula: Debt_to_Income_Ratio = Debt / (Income + 1e-5) (to prevent division by zero)\n# Usefulness: This feature provides insight into the individual's financial health. A higher ratio may indicate financial instability, which might impact an individual's likelihood of being approved for a credit card. This is a critical feature in financial decision-making.\ndf['Debt_to_Income_Ratio'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1e-5)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature Name: Employment_Duration_Normalized\n# Feature Description: Normalize the YearsEmployed column using z-score normalization to scale it relative to its distribution.\n# Formula: Employment_Duration_Normalized = zscore(YearsEmployed)\n# Usefulness: By normalizing the employment duration, potential correlations between job stability (tenure) and credit card approval can be captured more effectively by the downstream model, without being influenced by the scale of the original values.\ndf['Employment_Duration_Normalized'] = (df['YearsEmployed'] - df['YearsEmployed'].mean()) / df['YearsEmployed'].std()\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature Name: Age_Marital_Status_Interaction\n# Feature Description: Capture an interaction term between Age and Married. This feature multiplies the age by marital status (1 for married and 0 for not married).\n# Formula: Age_Marital_Status_Interaction = Age * Married\n# Usefulness: Married individuals might exhibit different credit card approval behaviors based on their age, due to life circumstances like family responsibilities or dual incomes. This interaction can model such non-linear associations effectively.\ndf['Age_Marital_Status_Interaction'] = df['Age'] * df['Married']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\n# The ZipCode column is highly granular and unlikely to contribute meaningful insights to the credit card application outcome. It may also introduce noise into the model due to its variability and lack of clear association with target labels.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
