loyment_stability" is dropped\n# The "employment_stability" column is redundant because it overlaps conceptually with "YearsEmployed". \n# Additionally, "YearsEmployed" provides more granular information compared to the bins formed in "employment_stability".\n\ndf.drop(columns=[\'employment_stability\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column "age_bin" is dropped\n# The "age_bin" column is categorical and represents an oversimplification of "Age". Since "Age" is already a numerical feature and can be directly analyzed or binned for specific use cases, the "age_bin" column is unnecessary.\n\ndf.drop(columns=[\'age_bin\'], inplace=True) \n```end', 'Here are the three possible feature engineering steps to improve predictions for whether individuals successfully apply for a credit card:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Debt-to-Age Ratio\nCreate a new feature that captures the average burden of debt relative to the applicant’s age. Younger individuals with high debt may exhibit different credit behaviors compared to older individuals.\n\n```python\n# debt_to_age_ratio\n# Feature description: The ratio of an individual\'s debt to their age.\n# Usefulness: Captures how burdened the individual is with debt relative to their stage of life. Helps to detect individuals who may be overleveraged (e.g., younger individuals with high debt may be at higher risk of default or rejection). This feature introduces context by combining "Debt" and "Age".\ndf[\'debt_to_age_ratio\'] = df[\'Debt\'] / df[\'Age\']\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Employment Income Stability\nCombine "YearsEmployed" and "Income" to create a new feature that measures income stability in relation to employment duration. This could help identify individuals with stable financial situations.\n\n```python\n# employment_income_stability\n# Feature description: A measure of income earned per year of employment.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures financial stability over time by dividing income by years 